WEBVTT

NOTE English visual guide. Editorial descriptions of on-screen concepts; no spoken narration.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Text preparation turns a sequence of symbols into vectors that the Transformer can process.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.200
The teaching example uses Tiny Shakespeare and compares different choices of token unit.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.400
Word tokenization uses whole words, but creates a larger vocabulary and needs a policy for unseen

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.800
words.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.200
Subword tokenization can represent words using reusable pieces.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Byte-pair encoding repeatedly merges frequent neighboring units, using rules learned from the

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
training text.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.600
The Shakespeare example illustrates a learned BPE vocabulary and its effect on sequence length.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.200
The reference comparison uses 65 character tokens, 12,571 word entries or 265 BPE entries. Counts

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.400
depend on the tokenizer and split.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.800
Larger vocabularies increase the embedding table and output vocabulary projection. The character

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.600
table here has 65×64 = 4,160 entries.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.600
An ID selects one row from the embedding table. This converts each token into a 64-dimensional

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.200
starting vector.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.400
Position information distinguishes identical tokens at different locations. This model learns 64

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.800
position vectors of width 64.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.600
Split training and validation text before making windows, preventing windows from crossing the split

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.200
and leaking validation text.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.400
The input is a text window. The target is the same stream shifted one token ahead, so each position

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.800
predicts its successor.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.600
A batch groups 32 windows of 64 token IDs. Input and target tensors have shape 32×64 before

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.200
embedding.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.800
Pause: why split before sampling windows? What grows when vocabulary size grows?

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.800
Splitting first keeps validation content out of training. Both the embedding table and output

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.600
projection grow with vocabulary size.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.040
Recap: tokenize, look up vectors, include positions, split carefully, and create shifted

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.080
input–target windows.
